but only through a hub.** 1330\u2192678\u219228\u2192361 (steps 07, 08, 09) and 1330\u2192669\u2192199\u219228\u2192361 (steps 11, 12, 09). Both funnel through node 28.\n- **Node 28 is the confounder.** It is the graph's dominant hub \u2014 1204 events across 746 distinct partners (steps 10, 14), roughly 7.5% of all 16,035 events (step 01). In a 2000-node graph, almost *any* pair is 2\u20133 static hops apart via 28; the 2-hop neighbourhoods of both endpoints (steps 05, 06) are essentially 28's partner list. Static reachability through such a hub is exactly the \"connection when time is ignored\" that the task warns is insufficient.\n- **The relay edges are single, non-repeating events.** 1330\u2192678, 678\u219228, 669\u2192199 each fire exactly once (steps 07, 08, 11), and the endpoints are low-activity (1330: 7 events; 361: 10 \u2014 step 02; repeat ratio 0.0423 overall, step 01). A cascade would need these isolated one-shot events to land in the correct order with every gap under 600 units \u2014 a narrow coincidence I have no means to confirm or refute.\n\nAbsent timestamps I cannot distinguish a genuine time-respecting chain from hub-mediated static co-occurrence, so I decline to assert either \"present\" or \"absent.\"\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_dc3dfaa5\",\n  \"decision\": \"insufficient\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [1330, 361, 678, 28, 669, 199],\n  \"window\": [4765, 43165],\n  \"confidence\": 0.7,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\":